title: Pushed by a build role, signed by the registry
subtitle: The signature attests to the pipeline, not to a laptop - because signing follows whoever pushed
width: 1100
---
$ aws codebuild start-build --project-name week22-imagesec-build

  === pushing clean    at 04:32:27Z ===
  === pushing vuln-os  at 04:32:31Z ===
  === pushing vuln-lib at 04:32:42Z ===

  [ok] build SUCCEEDED

Nothing in the build signs anything. ECR does it, because the registry
carries a signing rule and the pushing identity is allowed to use it.

$ aws ecr describe-image-signing-status \
      --repository-name wk22-app --image-id imageDigest=sha256:1fbdb597...

  {
    "signingProfileArn": ".../signing-profiles/wk22_4739d091f26b94721bddec3e9f",
    "status": "COMPLETE"                                              [ok]
  }

The signature is a separate artifact in the same repository, untagged:

$ aws ecr describe-images --repository-name wk22-app \
      --query 'imageDetails[].{tags:imageTags,type:artifactMediaType}'

  clean-20261006-053839    application/vnd.docker.container.image.v1+json
  <untagged>               application/vnd.cncf.notary.signature         <- the signature
  vuln-os-20261006-053839  application/vnd.docker.container.image.v1+json
  <untagged>               application/vnd.cncf.notary.signature

Each one counts against the repository's image quota. A lifecycle rule
expiring untagged images does NOT delete them - reference artifacts are
tied to their subject image. Confirmed with the preview API rather than
assumed:

$ aws ecr start-lifecycle-policy-preview --repository-name wk22-app
$ aws ecr get-lifecycle-policy-preview   --repository-name wk22-app

  "expiringImageTotalCount": 0                                        [ok]

The permission that is easy to miss: the build role needs signer:SignPayload
as well as the ECR push actions. Without it the push still succeeds and the
image is simply never signed - no error, anywhere.
